In the realm of electron-atom scattering, **Coupled-Channel (CC)** equations have proven to be indispensable tools for describing interactions. Their application has been particularly successful within the **Convergent Close-Coupling (CCC)** approach, implemented by **Bray and Stelbovics** [9], a methodology later extended to atoms with one or two valence electrons outside an inert core [10, 11, 13].

To execute these sophisticated calculations, we rely on the **reduced matrix elements** of the coupled-channel potentials. These elements, denoted by $R_{\alpha\beta}$, encapsulate the complete set of quantum numbers specifying a particular configuration of the system under study.

Specifically, for electron scattering from an **-electron target**, the reduced matrix elements exhibit a distinct structure comprising both direct and exchange terms:

$$R_{\alpha\beta} = \langle \alpha | V | \beta \rangle_{\text{CC}}$$

In this expression:
*   $\alpha$ and $\beta$ represent the target electron states;
*   $V$ denotes the potential operator;
*   $\langle \alpha | V | \beta \rangle_{\text{CC}}$ represents the complete set of quantum numbers defining the specific system configuration;
*   $T$ is the **permutation operator**;
*   $E_{\text{sys}}$ is the **system energy**;
*   The subscript **0** identifies the **incoming electron**;
*   The subscripts **1–n** denote the **target electrons**.

By utilizing **explicitly antisymmetrized target states**, these terms simplify further into the following form:

$$R_{\alpha\beta} = \frac{1}{(n-\alpha+1)(n-\beta+1)} \sum_{k=0}^{n-\alpha} \sum_{l=0}^{n-\beta} \frac{1}{E_{\text{sys}} - E_{k,l}} \langle \alpha | \frac{1}{k-l} | \beta \rangle_{\text{CC}}$$

Each of these terms will be derived explicitly for **e-He** and **e-Li** scattering in **Sections 3.2 and 3.3**.

A Comprehensive Review of Gastrointestinal Hemorrhage (GIH) in Adult Patients from September 1998 to August 1999

This study aimed to identify and analyze all adult patients admitted to our hospital's Emergency Department (ED) for GIH between September 1998 and August 1999. To ensure rigorous data collection, we identified every admitted patient from our hospital databases and obtained their medical records for data abstraction.

We meticulously applied strict exclusion criteria:
🔹 Age under 18 years;
🔹 Direct admission from a physician's office or another hospital;
🔹 History of previous inflammatory bowel disease;
🔹 Previous enrollment in this specific study.

All data were collected using a standardized uniform data sheet. To ensure high-quality results, two independent investigators abstracted the data, and 10% of charts were reviewed to verify inter-rater reliability. This rigorous approach allowed us to derive accurate insights into the characteristics of GIH during this critical period.
